2. Production shared-daemon coverage. scripts/e2e/shared_codex.py now invokes capability_scan.NativeScanner on the real installed Codex shared daemon, actual --codex-proxy companions and actual daemon-started bboxy450-term mcp bridge children. Warm/direct and cold/forwarding-shim runs each scan before and after generation replacement. Four snapshots contain 20 process observations and 60 source checks, all zero for the issued 64-hex values and BBOXY_CAPABILITY=. MCP bridge and daemon-started-child are overlapping roles of the same real processes, not invented separate PIDs. Each snapshot includes one daemon, two companions and two bridges. Values from retired generations remain only in scanner RAM: each mode checks two values before replacement and three after it.
The scanner checks ps -Eww and the actual owned process's KERN_PROCARGS2 exec-environment region, then runs /usr/bin/env with that kernel snapshot. Darwin's auxiliary string entries are conservatively retained, not filtered out; zero for this superset implies zero for the exec environment. It does not ask the closed-source daemon to execute env internally. A private HOME sentinel and process stamps prevent a redacted/empty or replaced snapshot from counting as success. No observer-environment fallback exists. Only owned fixture PIDs are traversed and only counts/roles/PIDs survive. test-capability-scan uses real owned children with known argv/environment, plus a real legacy-value negative case, to validate the reader and failure path. No kernel implementation code was copied; the independent ABI parser cites Apple xnu's kern_sysctl.c.
The existing four-kind fixture remains and produces its 36 seat/ancestor/env scans; the new 60 checks cover the previously missing real shared-daemon path. Both real SDK modes still authenticate orient, resume after restart, preserve their borrowed daemon after seat/app close, and make zero external model calls with zero operator input.

3. Exclusive overlay. capability::create_overlay creates parents separately and uses non-recursive mkdir for the final launch-ULID component. Existing directories and symlinks get typed refusals, never reuse. The directory is opened with O_DIRECTORY|O_NOFOLLOW and fstat checks directory type, exact 0700 mode and euid ownership. seat_wiring::overlay_creation_refuses_existing_symlinks_and_directories pre-creates a symlink, verifies the typed reason, and proves the unrelated target and link remain untouched; it also checks directory reuse refusal and successful overlay mode.

4. Failed-plan cleanup. An owned Overlay guard removes an unretained overlay when planning returns an error and transfers cleanup to the existing lifecycle only on success. The tests cover both Claude --bare rejection after creation and an actual Claude wiring write failure. The latter is an owned subprocess with RLIMIT_FSIZE=65: capability creation fits, subsequent wiring returns EFBIG, and FailedWiringRemovesOwnedOverlay asserts the directory is gone. The cleanup model adds AbandonFailedPlan; the existing omitted-cleanup control violates the same ownership invariant on both failure and normal retirement paths. Active same-uid attacks remain outside the ICD boundary.

5. Stale docs. Updated the four paths explicitly named in the authenticated rejection: crates/bbt-mux/README.md, .bboxy/specs/architecture.md, harness-kits.md and w2-5-mcp-and-seat-wiring.md. They now describe path-only environment delivery and the validated overlay file. No legacy value-in-env description remains in these paths.

6. Lowercase format. The reader accepts only the writer's canonical lowercase hexadecimal form; capability_file_refuses_uppercase_hex checks the typed malformed refusal.

